        "tag": "main",
        "text": "LANGUAGE ONLINE"
      },
      {
        "y": 120.2,
        "x0": 130.5,
        "x1": 401.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Some languages do not easily accept words from other languages into their lexicon1, but"
      },
      {
        "y": 129.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 405.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "English has always welcomed them. It is estimated that English vocabulary has its sources2"
      },
      {
        "y": 139,
        "x0": 130.5,
        "x1": 393.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "in at least 120 languages. Some languages have, of course, provided English with more"
      },
      {
        "y": 147.2,
        "x0": 130.5,
        "x1": 408.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "words than others. English started out with a basic Anglo-Saxon3 word stock4. Viking5 and"
      },
      {
        "y": 156.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 407,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Norman6 invaders from the 9th century onwards enriched7 the language enormously with"
      },
      {
        "y": 166,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 408.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "large numbers of words brought from their own languages.TheVikings brought new words"
      },
      {
        "y": 174.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 406.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "of Germanic origin while the Normans spoke a form of French. Both sets of invaders had an"
      },
      {
        "y": 183.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 398.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "enormous impact on8 English vocabulary, explaining why English may sometimes seem"
      },
      {
        "y": 192.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 400.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "to have several words for the same basic concept9. During the Renaissance of the 15th to"
      },
      {
        "y": 201.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 402.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "the 17th centuries, scholars introduced many words of classical origin10. And throughout"
      },
      {
        "y": 211,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 392.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "history, English speakers’contact with the world as explorers, scientists, traders, pirates"
      },
      {
        "y": 219.2,
        "x0": 130.4,
        "x1": 393,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "and holiday-makers has had linguistic consequences11 in a wealth of new words from"
      },
      {
        "y": 229,
        "x0": 130.5,
        "x1": 393.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "every part of the world that they reached.These words taken from other languages are"
      },
