      {
        "y": 629,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 517.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Some English words may look like words in your language but have a different meaning. Such words"
      },
      {
        "y": 640.6,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 506.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "are known as false friends, e.g. the German word Gift looks like the English word gift [present] but"
      },
      {
        "y": 652.6,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 520.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "actually means poison in German. The English word sympathetic resembles a word meaning, simply,"
      },
      {
        "y": 664.6,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 511.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "nice in many other European languages, but in English sympathetic has a much narrower meaning"
      },
      {
        "y": 677,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 502.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "[understanding and caring about someone else’s suffering]. Note also that the pronunciation of a word"
      },
      {
        "y": 689,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 484.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "borrowed into English may be quite different from its pronunciation in its language of origin."
      }
